artments\nSchool\nOrganization\nDean's Office & Executive Support Units\nDepartments\nGoverning Bodies\nMission Statement\nChairs and Professorships\nSustainability\nTalent Management & Diversity\nFamily Room\nOpen Positions\nPeople\nProfessors\nNew@CIT\nAcademic Staff\nNon-academic Staff\nPrizes & Awards\nZEITlab\nEx-post-Transparenz\nAlumni\nEIKON e.V.\nHurwitz Society\nVacation Seminar\nEvents\nFiMaTUM\nLocations\nTUM Campus Heilbronn\nParabola slide\nContact\nInternational\nStudies\nOutgoing\nStudies\nEurope\nWorldwide\nPartnerships\nFreemover\nScholarships\nInternship\nShort Stay\nThesis\nIncoming\nElectrical & Computer Engineering\nInformatics\nMathematics\nDouble Degree\nMentoring Programs\nCollaboration & Research\nGuests\nCooperations\nProjects\nSummer Schools\nOffers for Staff\nNews\nCIT Pulse \u2013 Student Survey\n150 Years of Electrical and Computer Engineering\nMEET&MATCH \u2013 Companies Meet Talents\nOpen Day\nGraduation Ceremony Bachelor & Master\nGraduation Ceremony Doctorate & Habilitation\nInternational Day\nNew@CIT\nNewIn Videos\nWissenschaft f\u00fcr jedermann\nHome\nSchool\nOrganization\nOrganization\nEfficient structures in management and administration within the TUM School of Computation, Information and Technology ensure efficient internal processes and thus support the academic staff.\nExecutive Board\nDepartments\nSchool Office\nCommittees\nExecutive Board\nThe School Executive Board manages the TUM School of Computation, Information and Technology. It consists of:\nDean\nHans-Joachim Bungartz\nDean's Office\nVice Deans\nAcademic & Student Affairs\nOliver Hayden\nResearch & Innovation\nRobert Wille\nInformation Management & Digitalization\nAnton Frank\nTalent Management & Diversity\nDonna Ankerst\nDepartment Heads\nDepartment of Mathematics\nOliver Junge\nManagement Mathematics\nDepartment of Computer Science\nMartin Bichler\nManagement Computer Science\nDepartment of Computer Engineering\nAndreas Herkersdorf\nManagement Computer Engineering\nDepartment of Electrical Engineering\nKai M\u00fcller\nManagement Electrical Engineering\nDepartments\nThe departments structure the school into four scientific areas:\nDepartment of Mathematics\nDepartment of Computer Science\nDepartment of Computer Engineering\nDepartment of Electrical Engineering\nSchool Office\nThe School Office provides centralized services, manage student affairs, and serve as the point of contact for our students and doctoral candidates. It consists of the following units:\nManagement\nAnton Frank\nIngrid Heiser\nRichard Wolf\nSchool Services\nSchool Services support the departments and chairs on a needs basis with centralized\nServices for the management of HR, finances and room allocation and\nServices in areas such as IT, purchasing, repair and copying services.\nManagement\nAnton Frank\nRichard Wolf\nAcademic & Student Affairs\nThe Academic & Student Affairs unit consists of the Academic Programs Office and the Graduate Center. The Academic Programs Office manages the operational and administrative aspects of student affairs. The Graduate Center is the service point for doctoral candidates at the School of Computation, Information and Technology.\nManagement\nIngrid Heiser\nClaudia Philipps\nThomas Stolte\nDean's Office & Executive Units\nCommittees\nSpecialized committees support the TUM School of Computation, Information and Technology in its strategic positioning to keep apace of global development, to remain internationally competitive and highly attractive for students and staff in research and administration.\nNews from School\nSchool\n|\n27.11.2026\nhackaTUM\nThe hackathon organized by the Department of Computer Science at the Technical University of Munich is taking place for the 11th time this year. Since then, hackaTUM has developed into one of the largest hackathons in Europe. The event will take place from November 27 to 29, 2026.\n[read more]\n150 Years Electrical and Computer Engineering\n|\n25.11.2026\nData, Sensors and Quantum Science at \"Wissenschaft f\u00fcr jedermann\"\nWhile the first lecture on electrical telegraphy laid the foundation in 1876, the field has since evolved into a broad spectrum encompassing more than 40 chairs and professorships. Four TUM professors offer insights into their research on November 25, 2026 at the Deutsches Museum.\n[read more]\nStudies,\nSchool\n|\n28.10.2026\nBeyond Dots and Loops \u2013 Keynote by Mair\u00e9ad Pratschke\nDomain Expertise or Domain Intelligence? On October 28, 2026, at 6:30 p.m., Mair\u00e9ad Pratschke will discuss which skills are needed in the age of autonomous AI systems and how we must learn, teach and work to remain relevant in an increasingly uncertain future.\n[read more]\nResearch,\nSchool\n|\n26.10.2026\nLarge Scale Dynamics Conference: Quantum, Classical & Stochastic\nFrom October 26 to 29, 2026, the Large Scale Dynamics Conference will bring together experts in quantum, classical and stochastic dynamics. Look forward to exciting plenary talks and poster presentations at the TUM Institute for Advanced Study on the Garching Research Campus.\n[read more]\nAwards\n|\n23.10.2026\nTUM Distinguished Affiliated Professorship for Ulrich L. Rohde\nFrom high-frequency technology to supporting young talent: With the Distinguished Affiliated Professorship, TUM honors Ulrich L. Rohde for a lifetime of work that uniquely combines science, entrepreneurship, and a spirit of innovation.\n[read more]\nTo top\nPrivacy\nImprint\nAccessibility", "links": [["Skip to content", "https://www.cit.tum.de/en/cit/school/organization/#content"], ["de", "https://www.cit.tum.de/cit/school/organisation/"], ["", "https://www.cit.tum.de/en/cit/datenschutz/#GoogleCustomSearch"], ["TUM School of Computation, Information and Technology", "https://www.cit.tum.de/en/cit/home/"], ["Technical University of Munich", "https://www.tum.de/en/"], ["", "https://www.tum.de/en/"], ["Home", "https://www.cit.tum.de/en/cit/home/"], ["Studies", "https://www.cit.tum.d